Let the original price of the watch be $P = \$2000$.
Mr. Rogers bought the watch at 75% off the original price, so the price he paid is $P_1 = P - 0.75P = 0.25P = 0.25 \times \$2000 = \$500$.
Mr. Rogers sold the watch to his friend at 120% off the price he paid. So the selling price is $P_2 = P_1 + 1.20P_1 = 2.20P_1 = 2.20 \times \$500 = \$1100$.
The percentage discount obtained by Mr. Rogers' friend is the difference between the original price and the selling price, divided by the original price, multiplied by 100%.
Discount percentage = $\frac{P - P_2}{P} \times 100\% = \frac{\$2000 - \$1100}{\$2000} \times 100\% = \frac{\$900}{\$2000} \times 100\% = 0.45 \times 100\% = 45\%$.

The question asks for the percentage discount obtained by Mr. Rogers' friend from the original price.
The original price is $P = \$2000$.
The selling price to his friend is $P_2 = \$1100$.
The discount is $P - P_2 = \$2000 - \$1100 = \$900$.
The percentage discount from the original price is $\frac{900}{2000} \times 100\% = 0.45 \times 100\% = 45\%$.

